  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1223 AM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nWidespread rain Friday into Saturday\n\nWV satellite shows and upper high over the ArkLaTex region, a low \nmoving over northern CA, and a longwave trough advancing over \neastern Canada. Multiple shortwaves are seen within southwesterly \nflow aloft from the Four Corners to the northern Plains. At the \nsurface, lee pressure falls are occurring in NE CO with southerly \nreturn flow in the southern Plains to the east of this feature, a \nhigh is building in western Ontario in the wake of the Canadian \nupper trough, and a quasi-stationary front is present roughly along \nInterstate 70 where the northeasterly winds associated with the \nOntario high meet the northern extent of the return flow.\n\nFriday into Saturday, aforementioned front will tighten as it is \nforced northward by the strong return flow across the central CONUS. \nBroad lift across the area looks to occur by mid-morning as a \ndisturbance ejects over MN so could see a few lighter showers early. \nAs time goes on, strong return flow will lead to PWATs approaching \n2\", near the top of climatology. This return flow will also serve to \nboth force the front northward and tighten the front. As 850/700mb \nfrontogenesis increases over the CWA in the afternoon, additional \nshowers will occur. Over time, the front should stall out over the \nforecast area and, as a LLJ ramps up during the evening, rounds of \nmoderate to heavy rain will occur with a few rumbles of thunder as \nelevated instability increases. Overall, 17.19z NBM QPF values \nsuggest most areas have a 50% chance for final rain totals to be \nwithin the 1-4\" range and a small (5-20%) chance for more than 5\", \nmainly in northeast Iowa north of US Highway 18. Taking a look at \nthe 17.12z HREF and 17.18z REFS to get a sense of high end \npossibilities, probability-matched mean values of 5-7+\" are seen \nbetween I-90 and a latitude defined by the IA/MN border. Needless to \nsay, this would lead to flooding issues for affected basins and \naffected paved areas, as discussed further in the Hydrology section \nbelow.\n\nCooler with additional shots at rain next week\n\nThe front that fills the focus of all our rain problems Friday \ninto Saturday looks to be shunted back southward as the Ontario \nsurface high continues to build. This will lead to cooler \ntemperatures through next week. Quasi-zonal flow aloft favors \nperiodic potential for rain as disturbances translate \ndownstream. With the aforementioned front favored to be to our \nsouth, best chance for additional rain during each of these \nperiods looks to be in northeast Iowa and southwest Wisconsin. \nNo periods stand out as having a higher amount of hazard but, \ndepending on where heaviest rain falls Friday/Saturday, any \nadditional rain in NE IA/SW WI would be unwelcome and delay \nfalls in rivers.",
